course: euphemisms
lesson: 28
title: Soft words for hard things: crime, prison and the police and lying and honesty
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Stealing, killing and lying are things people are ashamed of, so they hide them behind softer words.
explain: Officials use long words that sound like paperwork: stealing money becomes "misappropriation of funds", fraud becomes "irregularities", a lie becomes an "inoperative statement".
explain: Ordinary people joke instead: stolen goods "fell off the back of a lorry", and a liar is "telling porkies".
explain: Some soft words are kind, like a "white lie" told to spare someone's feelings; others are spin that hides a real lie.
explain: A vivid example: a politician who says he "misremembered" may simply have lied.
fact: "Misappropriation of funds" means stealing money that one was trusted to look after, such as a club's treasurer (the person who looks after its money) taking it.
fact: "Irregularities" in official reports means fraud (cheating people out of money) or other wrongdoing.
fact: "Fell off the back of a lorry" is a British joke meaning the goods were stolen.
fact: "Fell off a truck" is the US joke meaning the goods were stolen.
fact: "Liberate" is a jokey way to say steal.
fact: "Sleep with the fishes" is US gangster slang for being murdered and dumped in water.
fact: "The ultimate penalty" means the death penalty: the law kills (executes) the criminal.
fact: "Under the influence" means drunk or on drugs.
fact: "Social engineering" means tricking people into giving away secrets such as passwords.
fact: "Enhanced pat-down" is a US official phrase for a thorough body search, for example at an airport.
fact: A "fib" is a small lie.
fact: A "white lie" is a harmless lie told to spare someone's feelings.
fact: "Stretching the truth" means exaggerating so much that it becomes lying.
fact: "Creative with the truth" is a jokey way to say lying.
fact: A "tall tale" is an invented, unbelievable story.
fact: "Telling porkies" is British slang for telling lies, from the rhyming slang "porky pies" for lies.
fact: "Telling stories" is what British adults say to a child who is lying.
fact: "Misremember" is a US official way to say someone remembered wrongly or lied about the past.
fact: An "inoperative statement" is a US political phrase for a statement now admitted to be false.
fact: "Strategic misrepresentation" means deliberate lying to win approval, for example hiding a project's real cost.
exercise: What is "misappropriation of funds"? | Stealing money one was trusted with | Putting money in the wrong drawer | Spending money on a holiday | Forgetting where money was kept
exercise: An audit (a careful check of a company's money records) found "irregularities" in the accounts. What does it mean? | Fraud or wrongdoing | Messy handwriting | Numbers not in a straight line | Pages out of order
exercise: A Briton says a cheap TV "fell off the back of a lorry". What does it mean? | It was stolen | It was dropped and broken | It was delivered by lorry | It was found on the road
exercise: In US talk, what does "it fell off a truck" mean about a cheap watch? | It was stolen | It was damaged | It was a gift from a driver | It was made in a factory
exercise: "We liberated some biscuits from the staff room," a pupil jokes. What did they do? | Stole biscuits | Freed biscuits from a cage | Bought biscuits | Shared biscuits fairly
exercise: In gangster films, what does it mean when someone will "sleep with the fishes"? | He will be murdered and dumped in water | He will go on a long fishing trip with the gang | He will sleep on a boat | He will become a sailor
exercise: What is "the ultimate penalty"? | The death penalty | The last kick in a football match | The biggest parking fine | Being sent off for good
exercise: What does "under the influence" mean? | Drunk or on drugs | Under a friend's control | Hiding under a table | Following a famous person online
exercise: What is "social engineering" in computer security? | Tricking people into giving away secrets | Building houses for poor people | Planning a city's roads | Making friends at school
exercise: What is an "enhanced pat-down" at a US airport? | A thorough body search | A friendly pat on the back | A free massage | A quick wave through
exercise: What is a "fib"? | A small lie | A small bone | A big joke | A short story book
exercise: What is a "white lie"? | A harmless lie told to spare feelings | A lie written on white paper | A lie that hurts someone | A lie told by a ghost
exercise: What does "stretching the truth" mean? | Exaggerating so much it becomes lying | Telling a very long true story about your holiday | Doing yoga while talking | Speaking slowly
exercise: Someone is "creative with the truth". What are they doing? | Lying | Painting true pictures | Rhyming | Inventing a machine
exercise: What is a "tall tale"? | An invented, unbelievable story | A story about tall people | A story printed in big letters | A true story about giants
exercise: In Britain, what is "telling porkies"? | Telling lies | Talking about pigs | Cooking | Telling jokes at a party
exercise: A British mum says, "Don't tell stories, young man." What does she mean? | Stop lying | Stop reading books | Stop talking at bedtime | Stop singing
exercise: An official says he "misremembered" the meeting. What may he really have done? | Lied about it or recalled it falsely | Forgot to go to it | Remembered every word of it perfectly | Recorded it on video
exercise: What is an "inoperative statement"? | A statement now admitted to be false | A statement made in a hospital | A statement that is too quiet to hear | A statement about broken machines
exercise: What is "strategic misrepresentation"? | Deliberate lying to win approval | A clever drawing of a map | A mistake made by accident | Playing a game of strategy
test: News: "The charity's treasurer is accused of misappropriation of funds." What is she accused of? | Stealing money she was trusted to look after | Counting the money badly | Giving money to the poor | Losing the receipts
test: "Cheap trainers, mate, don't ask — they fell off the back of a lorry." What is the seller hinting? | The trainers are stolen | The trainers are damaged | The trainers came by post | The trainers are made of rubber
test: Teacher's report: "Sam has been telling stories about his homework again." What has Sam done? | Lied about his homework | Written great stories for homework | Read books aloud in class | Forgotten his homework once
test: Gran opens a gift she dislikes and says "Oh, lovely!" What kind of lie is that? | A white lie | A tall tale | Misappropriation | An inoperative statement
test: Bank warning: "Fraudsters use social engineering to get your PIN." What do they do? | Trick you into telling them your PIN | Build a fake bank | Fix your phone for free and then send you a big bill | Hack the bank's wires
test: "Oi, stop telling porkies! You never saw a shark in the canal." What is the speaker saying? | Stop lying | Stop eating pies | Stop talking about pigs | Hush
test: Old crime film: "Cross the boss and you'll sleep with the fishes." What is the threat? | You will be murdered | You will be sent fishing | You will lose your job | You will sleep on a boat
test: A driver was "under the influence" when he crashed. Which is true? | He was drunk or on drugs | He was being bossed by a friend | He was under a bridge | He was very tired
test: Press office: "The minister's earlier statement is inoperative." What does it admit? | The earlier statement was false | The minister was ill | The microphone was broken | The statement was too long
test: "My uncle caught a fish THIS big, he says. Classic tall tale." What does the speaker think? | The uncle's story is invented | The uncle is very tall | The uncle caught a real big fish | The uncle tells true stories
test: "He's being a bit creative with the truth about why he was late." What does it mean? | He is lying about why he was late | He drew a picture of his excuse | He has a clever true excuse | He was late because he was painting
test: A report finds the railway's costs were hidden by "strategic misrepresentation". What happened? | The real costs were hidden on purpose to win approval | The costs were counted by mistake | A map was drawn wrongly | The railway won a strategy prize
test: "Under the law of that country the ultimate penalty still exists." What does the law still allow? | Executing criminals | Huge fines for parking | Banning players for life | Sending people abroad
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the term "irregularities" as employed in an official audit? | It denotes fraud or wrongdoing | It denotes untidy handwriting | It denotes pages bound out of sequence | It denotes figures printed in uneven columns
exam: Identify the meaning of an "enhanced pat-down" as conducted pursuant to airport security regulations. | A thorough search of the body | A courteous pat upon the shoulder | A complimentary massage | An expedited passage without inspection
exam: It is given that a witness states that he "misremembered" an event. Select the response that is consistent with the established usage of that term. | He recalled it falsely or lied about it | He recalled it with perfect accuracy | He failed to attend the event | He recorded the event in writing
exam: Ascertain the meaning of "stretching the truth". | Exaggerating to the point of lying | Narrating a lengthy true account | Speaking in a deliberately slow manner | Performing exercises whilst conversing
